+> The examples below use a hardcoded value so they stay readable, but in a real app load a randomly generated secret of at least 32 characters from an environment variable such as `process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD`, and never commit it to source control. A guessable passphrase (even one ≥32 characters) is not safe. The library rejects passwords shorter than 32 characters but cannot check their entropy.
+
+### Rotating the Password
+
+To change the password without signing out existing users, move the old one to `extraUnsealPasswords`:
+
+```js
+import { useSession } from "h3";
+
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD, // new password, used to seal
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD], // only used to open
+});
+```
+
+Cookies sealed with the old password still open. Each one is resealed with the new password on the user's next request, so the old password can be removed once those sessions have expired or been resealed. That takes `maxAge`, if set, plus the time until each user's next request. Sessions read from the `x-h3-session` header are not resealed, so they keep working with the old password until they expire, and without `maxAge` or `idleTimeout` they never expire.
+
+Keep the list to two passwords or fewer. Each one adds a decryption attempt to every request whose cookie the current password does not open. Like `password`, each one must be at least 32 characters; a shorter one cannot open any cookie, so its sessions are treated as new.
 
 This will initialize a session and return an header `Set-Cookie` with a cookie named `h3` and an encrypted content.
 
